# CODEOWNERS — Blueprint §12, KF-S0-OPS-001.
#
# Written before the code it governs. Most of these paths are empty or barely
# populated today; the rule exists now so the first commit into them is already
# covered rather than the rule arriving after the code it was meant to guard.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE CAN AND CANNOT DO
#
# The specification asks for "1 approval (2 for apps/api/src/modules/{billing,
# attendance}, shared-kernel/, and any prisma/migrations/** path via
# CODEOWNERS)". CODEOWNERS cannot express an approval COUNT — it names who owns
# a path. The count lives in branch protection, and GitHub applies one count to
# the whole branch, not a higher one for selected paths.
#
# So the two-reviewer rule needs one of:
#   · two distinct owning teams on those lines, so "require review from Code
#     Owners" demands an approval from each — the usual way to get to two; or
#   · a repository ruleset with a path condition, which can carry its own
#     required-approval count.
#
# Neither is possible on a single-account repository: `ghonem100` is a personal
# account, so there are no teams, and one person cannot supply two approvals.
# The paths are therefore listed here with their single owner, which is what is
# enforceable today, and the two-reviewer requirement is recorded as outstanding
# rather than quietly dropped. See the KF-S0-OPS-001 completion record.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Everything, unless a later and more specific rule overrides it.
# Last matching pattern wins, so order matters: broad first, narrow last.
*                                   @ghonem100

# --- Two reviewers intended (Blueprint §12) --------------------------------
# Money. BR-06…BR-10, integer minor units, and the trigger-maintained invoice
# totals; a rounding or allocation error here is a real financial error.
/apps/api/src/modules/billing/      @ghonem100

# Child custody. BR-01 pickup authorization and BR-11 alternation — the
# safety-critical path where a mistake means a child leaves with the wrong
# adult.
/apps/api/src/modules/attendance/   @ghonem100

# The tenancy kernel. The Prisma extension, the tenant context and the model
# manifest: the code that decides whether RLS is actually applied. ADR-010.
/apps/api/src/shared-kernel/        @ghonem100

# Migrations are irreversible in production and are where a tenant table ships
# without a policy. The migration linter gates the annotation; review gates the
# judgement.
/apps/api/prisma/migrations/        @ghonem100

# --- Also worth a second pair of eyes --------------------------------------
# The gates themselves. A weakened gate is invisible until something it should
# have caught reaches production, so changes to the gates are reviewed like the
# code they protect (workflow §4 rule 7: a wrong gate is fixed in its own PR
# with its own justification, never disabled to land unrelated work).
/tooling/migration-lint/            @ghonem100
/tooling/tenancy-manifest/          @ghonem100
/configs/eslint/                    @ghonem100
/.github/workflows/                 @ghonem100
/.github/CODEOWNERS                 @ghonem100

# The source of truth for the API. `packages/contracts` is transcribed from the
# approved API Contract; a change here that is not in that document is a change
# to the contract made in the wrong place.
/packages/contracts/                @ghonem100

# Approved design documents. Phase 0–9 output; amended by decision, not by
# drive-by edit.
/docs/                              @ghonem100
